<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imagawa Ujizane</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1538–1615</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imagawa Ujizane was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>, son and heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a>. After his father's death at Okehazama in 1560 he lost the loyalty of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and in 1568 and 1569 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunpu">Sunpu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> forced his surrender at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakegawa-castle">Kakegawa Castle</a>, ending the Imagawa as a power. He lived on as a poet and later served the Tokugawa as an expert in ceremony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imagawa-ujizane.jpg" alt="Imagawa Ujizane" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 未詳。 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BB%8A%E5%B7%9D%E6%B0%8F%E7%9C%9F%E5%83%8F%EF%BC%88%E5%80%8B%E4%BA%BA%E5%83%8F%EF%BC%89_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
